{
  "description": "The Legend rows Python draws for a request with explicit track slots (gbdraw/diagrams/circular/assemble.py::_sync_legend_table_for_circular_slots, gbdraw/diagrams/linear/assemble.py::_sync_legend_table_for_linear_slots). `slots` are the entries of the canonical request (diagramOptions.tracks.circularTrackSlots or linearTrackSlots): spec strings or slot objects. `legend` gives rows the Legend table holds before the slots (feature rows). `depthTracks` gives the request's Depth series (diagramOptions.depthTracks: label and color; a blank label is Python's default label), and a null `slots` is a Circular request without explicit slots, which names a row per series (OV-1198). `palette` gives the gc_content, skew_high and skew_low colors of the Generate. `rows` is the whole table in order, each row as [key, palette key of its fill, the slot that draws it (null: no explicit slot), fill]. A slot whose row has the name and the swatch of an earlier row draws no row of its own; it still takes its key, listed in `shared`, so the keys of the later slots do not depend on which rows are shared (the Legend draws names, numbered by tests/fixtures/legend_names.json, never keys). Python's answer is the keys and fills (tests/test_legend_row_facts.py reads the slots as the request decoder and the slot parsers do). The Web app's answer is the slot and the palette key it reads for each key of the displayed Result (services/track-legend-rows.js, tests/web/track-legend-row-colors.test.mjs); a palette key `depth` is a Depth series' row, filled by the series; a null palette key is a row the Web app claims no track for, so it keeps the color Python drew. The rows of a request without explicit slots are in legend_generated_rows.json.",
